00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
hello and welcome to a special  world ocean day edition of the daily  climate show  on today's programme how destructive  fishing practices  are releasing carbon into the atmosphere  and damaging supposedly protected areas  letting nature take over the solution to  sea level rise  on the gower peninsula  and the fishermen turning the tide on  plastic  in the mediterranean  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes  happening to our world right now and  meet those coming up with the solutions  well today we're marking world ocean day  and this year's theme is life and  livelihoods  not only do our oceans produce more than  50 of the planet's oxygen  they are home to most of the earth's  biodiversity  and billions of people depend on them  for their main  source of food and our oceans can help  combat global warming absorbing  around 30 percent of the carbon dioxide  produced  by humans but they are under threat  in a moment we'll be finding out how one  uk community is adapting  to rising sea levels but first there's a  warning today  that our seas are in a death spiral from  climate change  and destructive fishing greenpeace says  that the chemistry of our waters is  rapidly  changing as boats using fishing methods  such as bottom trawling  stir up carbon dioxide and deplete  already  low fish stocks well damaging fishing  practices  are a huge issue around the world as our  science correspondent thomas moore  reports the ocean is in a death spiral  says greenpeace  and they're on a mission to protect it  for six months they'll patrol marine  conservation zones along the south coast  looking out for destructive forms of  fishing factory trawlers with nets a  mile long  and boats that drag heavy equipment  along the fragile seabed  we're seeing the ocean facing more  pressures than ever before we're seeing  climate change we're seeing plastic  pollution we're seeing overfishing  and they're all interacting with each  other to create quite a dangerous  situation  earlier this year greenpeace found  evidence of unregulated fishing by  chinese trawlers in the indian ocean  some turn off their satellite trackers  to hide from authorities  it's likely to be happening in uk waters  too  this isn't just about fish stocks and  biodiversity it's about the climate  too protected areas in the uk's offshore  waters contain an estimated 26 and a  half  million tons of carbon and when fishing  gear is dragged along the bottom  all that gets stirred up  environmentalists and those trying to  eke out are living from small boats are  now often on the same  side martin barely catches enough fish  to pay for his fuel  i think we're facing uh an environmental  disaster here  we were catching sort of 30 odd boxes  and now we're down to around about a box  and a half  the government has plans to ban all  destructive activities in some  protected areas but greenpeace says more  needs to be done  more quickly to keep the ocean healthy  thomas more sky news in new haven  now one of the ways that we track the  impact of global warning on our planet  is of course through sea level rise it's  mainly caused by two factors both  related to global warming well the one  that we've often talked about on this  show is water from melting ice such as  glaciers and ice sheets  the second though is thermal expansion  water expands as it gets warmer so  as our oceans heat up those sea levels  rise  well global sea levels have risen  between 21 and  24 centimeters since the industrial  revolution  and crucially the rate of that rise is  accelerating  more than doubling from 1.4 millimeters  per year throughout most of the 20th  century  to 3.6 millimeters per year  from 2006 to 2015.  and the impact of those rising sea  levels is already being felt in  the uk in 2014 the sea wall at kamivi on  the gower peninsula in wales  suffered severe damage but rather than  rebuild it  the national trust let nature take over  as sky stan whitehead reports  when i came down to site and the site  that had been an agricultural field the  previous day  was you know three foot under sea water  seven years ago  the sea wall defending this farmland on  the gower peninsula  gave way but rather than rebuild  the national trust controversially at  the time  let the water take over we don't defend  against the sea you know we don't do it  we understand that  you know habitats need to be dynamic you  know things need to be able to change  the results an abundance of salt marsh  brimming with flower and fauna  and locking in carbon most people can  see  that we've got really great nature here  you know if you come down on a quiet  morning you'll be able to see an otter  over 300 years or so there have been  attempts to repair this sea wall but  that effort has now stopped and there  are many  who believe that letting nature take its  course  is actually the best way to enhance  environments like this  we know that we're going to lose nearly  1200 hectares of intertidal area  just from coastal squeeze and coastal  pressure by the end of the century  so we need these small areas where we're  giving some land back to nature  letting the sea take over here has  brought in new life  and while holding the line against  rising tides is needed in built-up  coastal areas easing the pressure  elsewhere  can create new landscapes for years to  come  dan whitehead sky news kumaivi well with  me now is jacqueline  savitz chief policy officer for north  america at the conservation organization  oceana very good afternoon to you  jacqueline we've had  more countries pledging to protect 30  of the oceans great news how far are we  away from that being a reality well i  think we have some work to do  i know a number of countries are aiming  to protect 30  by 2030 um and i believe that's very  much possible especially if we  make sure that those protections are  meaningful enforceable protections for  our oceans it'll allow us  to rebuild our fisheries and protect the  ocean ecosystems  protections though do come at a cost  don't they so how long will it take  before the benefits  of that kind of protection will outweigh  the costs  well you know i think it depends on  different fisheries um what we've found  is that if we rebuild fisheries we can  actually feed  a billion people a seafood meal every  day  that's obviously a goal we want to  aspire to especially with food  insecurity  and so sustainable fisheries actually  has benefits not just for  food security but for livelihoods for  the economy  for the environment and that's something  we obviously want to work towards  yeah and that's what today is all about  isn't it and we saw also the  damaging impact of bottom trawling on  the oceans why not just ban it outright  um well it depends on where you are in  some areas bottom trawling may be  perfectly fine but in areas where we  have  bottoms that have high habitat values  they're the nursery areas there's  spawning areas  for fish those are the areas where we  want to prevent bottom trawling and that  will  be done we've seen that done in many  cases and that will allow  fisheries to thrive we also saw  jacqueline  in the other report that we were showing  some of the benefits  of working with rising sea levels jackie  and how  how far are we in adapting to that as a  reality  well i think you know adaptation is is  something that we will be able to do in  some cases and not in others  um and that's why it becomes so  important that we mitigate the impacts  of climate change that we address that  carbon dioxide emissions  um which you mentioned the oceans are  absorbing a third of what we're emitting  but it's making them sick in the process  and the only way  to stop that sort of runaway train  of climate change uh sea level rise  ocean acidification  is to stop releasing so much carbon  dioxide into the atmosphere and that  means moving away from the use of fossil  fuels  and shifting to clean energy which  doesn't lead to sea level rise  well jacqueline sava it's been  fascinating talking to you happy world  ocean day thank you for your time  thanks samia the same to you  well you can find out more about world  ocean day and indeed why it matters as  well as how important the sea is in the  fight against climate change that's on  our website  you just need to head to skynews.com  climate well let's take a look at some  of the day's other climate news now and  boris johnson has told cabinet ministers  that this week's g7 meeting is a chance  to reinvigorate  the international community to tackle  climate change  leaders from the uk us canada france  italy  germany and japan will meet to discuss  global issues including  the climate crisis and on friday we'll  have a special  g7 edition of the daily climate show  live  from cornwall and you can see that here  on sky news  at 6 30 p.m a campaign group is calling  on the government to  rewild a tenth of national parks to  tackle the nature  and climate crises rewilding britain  says the process  which restores ecosystems to a more  natural state  could boost jobs and help the uk meet  its pledge to protect  30 percent of land by twenty thirty  and water voles are being released in  yorkshire in a bid  to save the endangered mammal the  project by yorkshire water  is part of their water works for  wildlife initiative  it's a 1.6 million pound scheme that  hopes to boost  natural habitats across the country  water voles have suffered a  steep decline in recent years as a  result of habitat loss water pollution  and predation by the invasive american  mink now for an ocean-themed climate  diary today we are hearing  from lefteris arapakis he founded  greece's first  professional fishing school to teach  more sustainable techniques  but also to encourage fishermen to  remove plastic from the mediterranean  sea  [Music]  i'm coming from a family of fishermen  actually my family hungry fishermen for  five generations  [Music]  one day i was talking with my father and  he was complaining that the fishermen  couldn't find enough personnel  for the fishing village i didn't know  anything about fishing  i think even today i'm the worst  fisherman in the country  [Music]  in the last three and a half years we  have trained more than 115  unemployed people with a greek fishing  ship  i was shocked to see that the fishermen  were connecting with their nets not only  fish  but also plastic lots of it  we started training fishermen from our  port to bring  plastic back in the lab the fishermen  cut this blast by accident  so we give them the motivation and the  system to bring it back to the land  we have collected already more than 110  000  kilos of plastic from the bottom of the  sea  plunge for the future is to be able to  replicate  this model in other parts of the world  we believe that we can make an actual  difference and a real impact in the  world  well that is everything from us for  today tomorrow  it's a battle bus but not as you know it  we're going to be finding out more about  the uk's first fully electric coach on a  zero  carbon tour that's at the same time  tomorrow here  on sky news hope to see you then  you

